### [bind()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Build the `Surv` response for the columns of `data`.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/greenwood/blob/main/greenwood/_outcome.py#L686-L729){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
bind(data)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Each argument is evaluated against `data` and the results go through `Surv()`,
`event_time()` (then `as_surv()`), or `first_event()`. Missing values are kept, as they are
by `Surv()`. To drop incomplete rows together with the covariates, pass the `Outcome` to an
estimator's `fit()` with `data=` instead.
:::

#### Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[data]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Any]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A data frame (pandas, Polars, PyArrow, DuckDB, a lazy frame, ...) or a mapping of
    column names to values.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Surv]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The response for this frame.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#5f7518aa .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

lung = gw.load_dataset("lung")
gw.Outcome.surv(time="time", event="status").bind(lung)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}
```
Surv(type=right, n=228, events=165)
```
:::
:::


### [event_time()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Describe an `event_time()` response by column names.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/greenwood/blob/main/greenwood/_outcome.py#L514-L552){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
event_time(time, status, time_max=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

When bound to data, the columns are passed to `event_time()`, so its validation applies:
status codes are `"e"` (exact), `"r"` (right-censored), `"l"` (left-censored), or `"i"`
(interval-censored, with the upper bound in `time_max`).
:::

#### Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[time]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The time column (the lower bound for interval-censored rows), or `duration(...)`.

<code>[status]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The column of status codes.

<code>[time_max]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column of interval upper bounds, missing for other rows.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Outcome]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A data-free description of the response.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#c05879fd .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

gw.Outcome.event_time(time="time", status="code", time_max="upper")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}
```
Outcome.event_time(time='time', status='code', time_max='upper')
```
:::
:::


### [first_event()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Describe a `first_event()` competing-risks response by column names.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/greenwood/blob/main/greenwood/_outcome.py#L554-L607){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
first_event(endpoints, *, censor_at=None, start=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

Each endpoint is a `(time, event)` pair of a time column and an event column or
comparison (such as `"pstat == 1"`), in priority order for ties. When bound to data, the
pairs are evaluated and passed to `first_event()`.
:::

#### Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[endpoints]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Mapping[str, tuple[str | Duration, str]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A mapping of state name to `(time, event)`.

<code>[censor_at]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column at which rows with no observed event are censored. The default is the
    latest endpoint time.

<code>[start]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The column of entry times, for late entry.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Outcome]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A data-free description of the response.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#337c007b .cell execution_count=7}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

gw.Outcome.first_event(endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")})
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=7}
```
Outcome.first_event(endpoints={'pcm': ('ptime', 'pstat'), 'death': ('futime', 'death')})
```
