### [surv()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}

::: {.doc-subject}

Describe a `Surv()` response by column names and expressions.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/greenwood/blob/main/greenwood/_outcome.py#L439-L512){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-Kind.FUNCTION}
```python
surv(time, time2=None, event=None, *, type=None, origin=0.0)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

The arguments are those of `Surv()` (and of R's `survival::Surv()`), written as they
would be inside a formula. When the `Outcome` is bound to data, each one is evaluated and
the results are passed to `Surv()`, so R's type inference and status rules apply: `1`/`2`
status passes through, `type=` selects left or interval data, and a factor gives a
multi-state response.
:::

#### Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[time]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The time column (or start time for counting data, or lower bound for interval data),
    or `duration(...)` for a time computed from two date columns.

<code>[time2]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | Duration | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The stop time for counting data, or the upper bound for interval data. As in R, a
    second argument without `event` is the status.

<code>[event]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   The status: a column, a comparison such as `"status == 2"`, or
    `"factor(cause, c(0, 1, 2), c('censor', 'pcm', 'death'))"` for a multi-state response.

<code>[type]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   As in `Surv()`: `"right"`, `"left"`, `"interval"`, `"counting"`, `"interval2"`, or
    `"mstate"`. The default infers it from the arguments.

<code>[origin]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.0]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Subtracted from every time, as in `Surv()`.
:::

#### Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [Outcome]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A data-free description of the response.
:::

#### Examples {.doc-examples}

::: {#45b1a9b2 .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import greenwood as gw

# pbc's status is 0 (censored), 1 (transplant), or 2 (died)
gw.Outcome.surv(time="time", event="status == 2")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=11}
```
Outcome.surv(time='time', event='status == 2')
```
:::
:::


